France|The reference point. Terroir-first, place-named rather than grape-named, and the source of the appellation model most of the world copied.
Italy|The most grape-diverse country on earth — over 350 authorized native varieties, and nearly every one of its twenty regions makes wine.
Spain|Third-largest producer, largest vineyard area. Tempranillo and Garnacha at the core, plus sherry, the world's most underpriced fine wine.
Portugal|Two fortified wines of global rank (Port, Madeira) and a native-grape catalogue almost entirely its own.
Germany|Riesling's homeland. Classification runs on ripeness (Prädikat) alongside the newer vineyard-based VDP hierarchy.
Austria|Grüner Veltliner and Blaufränkisch, plus Burgenland's botrytis sweet wines. Its DAC system is origin-typicity based.
Switzerland|Drinks nearly all of it domestically. Chasselas in the west, Merlot in Ticino, and rare alpine natives in the Valais.
Luxembourg|A small Moselle strip: Riesling, Auxerrois, Pinot Gris, and a serious Crémant.
Hungary|Tokaji is the historic peak — the first classified vineyard region in the world (1737).
Greece|Ancient roots, modern revival. Assyrtiko, Xinomavro and Agiorgitiko now travel internationally.
Romania|One of Europe's largest producers by volume, with native Feteascăs and the sweet Cotnari tradition.
Bulgaria|Mavrud and Melnik natives alongside a large Cabernet/Merlot export trade.
Moldova|Enormous vineyard area per capita; Purcari's reds are the flagship.
Georgia|The oldest continuous winemaking tradition known — roughly 8,000 years — and the qvevri amber-wine method.
Armenia|Areni Noir from Vayots Dzor, near the world's oldest known winery site (c. 4100 BC).
Azerbaijan|Madrasa and Bayanshira on the Caspian side of the Caucasus.
Croatia|Plavac Mali on the Dalmatian coast; Zinfandel's ancestor Crljenak Kaštelanski originates here.
Slovenia|Three distinct zones; Brda's Rebula and orange wines share a border and a style with Friuli.
Serbia|Prokupac-led revival plus Fruška Gora whites and the aromatized Bermet.
North Macedonia|Vranec-dominated; historically the wine cellar of Yugoslavia.
Montenegro|Vranac from around Lake Skadar, largely from one very large producer.
Bosnia and Herzegovina|Žilavka and Blatina in the Herzegovina limestone.
Albania|Ancient viticulture, small modern industry; Kallmet and the two Shesh grapes.
Kosovo|Rahovec is the historic centre, once a major bulk supplier.
Czechia|Moravia does the bulk of it — Austrian in style, with Pálava as a local creation.
Slovakia|Shares the Tokaj region with Hungary; Small Carpathians is the main belt.
Poland|A genuinely new industry built on cold-hardy hybrids as the climate shifts.
Ukraine|Odesa sparkling and Zakarpattia whites; Crimean production is under occupation.
Russia|Krasnodar and the Black Sea coast, with Abrau-Dyurso the historic sparkling house.
United Kingdom|Traditional-method sparkling on the same chalk as Champagne — now the country's calling card.
Belgium|Small but growing, mostly Germanic white varieties and Crémant.
Netherlands|A few hundred hectares of hybrids in the south, expanding with warming.
Denmark|Recognized as a wine-producing country by the EU in 2000; Solaris leads.
Sweden|Skåne in the far south; disease-resistant hybrids, tiny volumes.
Norway|Among the northernmost vineyards in the world, at roughly 59°N.
Ireland|A handful of vineyards; maritime climate makes it marginal.
Cyprus|Commandaria has documented lineage to the Crusades and is claimed as the world's oldest named wine.
Malta|Two small islands, two native grapes: Ġellewża and Girgentina.
Turkey|Fourth-largest vineyard area globally, though most grapes go to table and drying.
Lebanon|Bekaa Valley wine has survived war continuously; Château Musar is the international name.
Israel|Modern quality era began in the 1980s in Galilee and the Golan.
Palestine|Cremisan near Bethlehem works with indigenous Levantine varieties.
Jordan|A small industry around Mount Nebo and the northern highlands.
Syria|Bargylus in the Latakia hills continued production through the war.
United States|Fourth-largest producer. California is roughly 80 percent of it, but all 50 states now make wine.
Canada|Icewine is the signature export; Okanagan and Niagara are the quality centres.
Mexico|The oldest wine industry in the Americas (1597); Valle de Guadalupe drives the modern revival.
Argentina|Malbec at altitude. The Uco Valley's high-elevation sites are the current frontier.
Chile|Phylloxera-free, geographically isolated, and the only real home of Carmenère.
Uruguay|Tannat is the national grape, softer here than in its native Madiran.
Brazil|Sparkling wine is the strength; Vale do São Francisco harvests twice a year in the tropics.
Peru|Mostly feeds pisco production, but Ica makes still wine too.
Bolivia|Some of the highest vineyards on earth, above 2,000 m in Tarija.
Colombia|Near-equatorial viticulture with two harvests a year.
Venezuela|Carora in Lara state, tropical and irrigated.
Ecuador|Chota Valley, a rare equatorial wine zone.
Paraguay|Small production, mostly Tannat and hybrids.
Australia|Sixth-largest producer. Shiraz is the signature, and Rutherglen fortifieds are world-class rarities.
New Zealand|Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc changed the global market in a single generation.
South Africa|Wine since 1659. Old-vine Chenin Blanc and the Swartland movement define the modern era.
Morocco|The largest wine industry in the Maghreb, mostly reds and the pale Gris de Boulaouane.
Algeria|Once the world's largest wine exporter under French rule; a fraction of that today.
Tunisia|Muscat de Kelibia is the distinctive one; industry is small and coastal.
Egypt|Winemaking documented to the Pharaohs; today a very small commercial industry.
Ethiopia|Rift Valley plantings, a recent and deliberate investment.
Kenya|Naivasha near Lake Naivasha, at altitude on the equator.
Tanzania|Dodoma has produced wine since the 1930s, originally by missionaries.
Zimbabwe|A small industry, disrupted by land reform.
Namibia|Desert-edge viticulture around Omaruru.
Madagascar|Highland vineyards near Fianarantsoa, mostly hybrids.
China|Now among the largest vineyard areas in the world; Ningxia is the quality centre.
Japan|Koshu is the native white and the country's identity grape.
India|Nashik dominates; the industry is barely three decades old.
Thailand|The original New Latitude wine country, at 14°N with a managed dormancy cycle.
Vietnam|Da Lat highlands; mostly table-grape derived.
Myanmar|Two estates in Shan State at around 1,300 m.
Indonesia|Bali produces year-round with no dormancy — up to three harvests annually.
South Korea|Campbell Early dominates; small but established.
Taiwan|Subtropical viticulture in Changhua, two harvests per year.
Kazakhstan|Assa Valley near Almaty, on old Soviet-era plantings.
Uzbekistan|Long Silk Road grape history; mostly sweet and fortified styles.
Kyrgyzstan|Chüy Valley, very small scale.
Tajikistan|Sughd region, mostly sweet wines.
Turkmenistan|State-run production with fortified styles from Terbash.
Liechtenstein|A few dozen hectares in the Rhine valley, much of it belonging to the Princely family's own estate.
San Marino|A handful of producers on Monte Titano, working the same Sangiovese as neighbouring Romagna.
Cape Verde|Vines grown in volcanic ash inside the Fogo crater, watered by little more than sea mist.
Bhutan|The country's first commercial vineyards were planted in 2019, in Himalayan valleys above 2,000 m.
Cambodia|A tropical industry of a handful of estates near Battambang, harvesting twice a year.
Laos|French-planted vines around Vientiane, revived by a single estate on the Mekong.
Nepal|Very small Himalayan plantings; the monsoon, not the cold, is the limiting factor.
